In this lesson we'll look at sine and cosine through the unit circle. Picture a circle of radius one, centered at the origin, with a starting point on the right side at one comma zero. Now, take any angle, call it theta, and wrap it around anticlockwise from that start. If theta is positive you go up and left, if it's negative you go clockwise. The spot you land on is a point we'll call P. Sin of theta is simply the vertical coordinate of P, and cosine of theta is the horizontal coordinate. That's the whole definition. Now, here's the neat part. Because P sits on a circle of radius one, by good old Pythagoras its coordinates always satisfy the identity that the square of sine plus the square of cosine equals one. So sine and cosine can never sneak past one, and they never dip below negative one. They bounce between those bounds forever. Because we wrap around the whole circle, any angle that lands on the same point gives the same sine and cosine, even if it spins around many times or spins the other way. In just a moment we'll try a worked example to get the feel of it.